    "result_text": "There's only one test class in the project, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-country national-number length check for E.164 telephones in `src/main/java/.../rest/advice/E164Telephone.java` \u2014 the single file that owns telephone normalization.\n\nThe change:\n- Added a small private helper `nationalLengthOk(String digits)` that rejects `+61` numbers without exactly 9 national digits and `+1` numbers without exactly 10, staying permissive for other country codes.\n- Extended `toE164`'s existing return guard by one clause (`&& nationalLengthOk(digits)`) so an invalid length yields `null`, which the existing `OwnerFieldsValidationAdvice` already maps to a 400 rejection.\n\nStructural impact was kept low: only one file touched, the edit to the existing `toE164` was a single line, and the new behaviour lives in a cohesive one-purpose method inside this tiny class (near-zero surrounding complexity), rather than growing any large class or method. The existing valid-number path (10-digit AU mobiles \u2192 9 national digits under `+61`) still passes, and the new test rejecting `+61 123` returns 400.",
